   "variant_id": null,
   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let g be defined by g(x) = 2x + 3 for x < 1, g(1) = 2k^2 - 4k - 1, and g(x) = k^2 - kx - 1 for x > 1, where k is a constant. For what value of k is g continuous at x = 1?"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 3
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "The left-hand limit at x = 1 is 2(1) + 3 = 5 and the right-hand limit is k^2 - k - 1.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Equal",
            5,
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Power",
                  "k",
                  2
               ],
               [
                  "Negate",
                  "k"
               ],
               -1
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Equal one-sided limits need k^2 - k - 6 = 0, so k = 3 or k = -2.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Equal",
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Power",
                  "k",
                  2
               ],
               [
                  "Negate",
                  "k"
               ],
               -6
            ],
            0
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The common limit 5 must also equal g(1) = 2k^2 - 4k - 1. At k = -2, g(1) = 15, while at k = 3, g(1) = 5.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Equal",
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Multiply",
                  2,
                  [
                     "Power",
                     "k",
                     2
                  ]
               ],
               [
                  "Multiply",
                  -4,
                  "k"
               ],
               -1
            ],
            5
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "Only k = 3 makes g continuous at x = 1.",
         "mathjson": 3
      }
   ],
